For $B_{\rm reuse}$ equal-sized subsequent batches, model
\begin{equation}
 T_{\rm policy}(B_{\rm reuse})=T_{\rm cal}+B_{\rm reuse}T_{\rm chosen},\qquad
 T_{\rm fixed}(B_{\rm reuse})=B_{\rm reuse}T_f.
 \label{eq:6-total-cost}
\end{equation}
When $T_{\rm chosen}<T_f$, the break-even count is
\begin{equation}
 B_{\rm reuse}^*=\frac{T_{\rm cal}}{T_f-T_{\rm chosen}}.
 \label{eq:6-payback}
\end{equation}
Indeed,
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 T_{\rm policy}<T_{\rm fixed}
 &\iff T_{\rm cal}<B_{\rm reuse}(T_f-T_{\rm chosen})\\
 &\iff B_{\rm reuse}>B_{\rm reuse}^*
 \qquad(T_{\rm chosen}<T_f).
 \end{aligned}
\]
If $T_{\rm chosen}\geq T_f$, there is no throughput payback. Batch time is $T=1000n/Q$ milliseconds.
For varying choices, convexity gives $1/\mathbb E[Q]\leq\mathbb E[1/Q]$: inverse mean QPS understates mean time. \Cref{sec:a-budget-contract,sec:7-stream-cost} give boundaries and four direct cost/quality checks.
